ol | absent | Three implicit steps (read, parse, write) are described in prose without numbering, ordering, or completion criteria. |\n| 11 | Self-Correcting Loop | Workflow Control | absent | Input validation exists (\"not valid JSON\") but no post-write verification that the output is valid and was written successfully. |\n| 12 | Plan-Validate-Execute | Workflow Control | absent | Skill overwrites user files with no preview or approval gate; a formatting change to the wrong file is hard to undo without version control. |\n| 13 | Utility Bundle | Executable Code | N/A | Task is simple enough for LLM-native JSON parsing; no deterministic script needed. |\n| 14 | Autonomy Calibration | Meta | present | `user-invocable: true` is set, appropriate for a utility skill, but a file-modifying skill lacks any approval gate, which is a risk mismatch. |\n\n**Summary**: 4 of 11 applicable patterns present, 1 of 11 strong, 10 improvements suggested\n\n### Activation Test\n\n**Description analyzed**: \"Format JSON files\"\n\n**Would trigger on**:\n- \"Format this JSON file for me\"\n- \"Can you prettify my config.json?\"\n- \"Fix the indentation in data.json\"\n\n**Should NOT trigger on**:\n- \"Convert this YAML file to JSON\"\n\n**Observations**: The description is too terse for reliable routing. \"Format JSON files\" could be confused with JSON schema validation, JSON-to-other-format conversion, or general file formatting. Adding trigger verbs (\"prettify\", \"reindent\", \"normalize whitespace\"), an `argument-hint: <file-path>`, and an exclusion clause (\"Not for JSON validation, conversion, or schema checking\") would sharpen activation significantly.", "stop_reason": "end_turn", "session_id": "ebe76fed-75f6-46d2-b561-85216f4ed701", "total_cost_usd": 0.36298225000000006, "usage": {"input_tokens": 5, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 41465, "cache_read_input_tokens": 77902, "output_tokens": 2594, "server_tool_use": {"web_search_requests": 0, "web_fetch_requests": 0}, "service_ti